 M.write_text('# Task 12T — Structural Provenance, Authority, and Multi-Actor Analysis\n\n## Readiness\n\n**PROVENANCE MODEL READY FOR STRUCTURAL CONTRACT DESIGN.** This authorizes only a future design task, not implementation.\n\n## Core finding\n\nA source may assert a fact, a verifier may verify it, and a fact-specific authority may be allowed to establish it. These are different roles. Current ETHOS already provides immutable factual records, source/verification/reference/time/phase, conformance/admission separation, conflict retention, and pre-gate filtering. It does not express multi-actor subject/actor/assertor separation, fact-specific authority, lineage, authenticated source instance, swarm scope, or observation independence.\n\n## Multi-actor safety\n\nOne evidence model can scale from one participant to N participants if participant identities are scoped and relations are many-to-many. Assertor, observer, actor, requester, authorizer, responsible party, execution actor, target, and verifier cannot be collapsed. Hierarchies and joint actions require explicit sourced relations, never inferred responsibility displacement.\n\n## Unknown and neutrality\n\nConflicts, unauthenticated identity, missing lineage, unknown independence, or insufficient authority remain UNKNOWN. Neutral shared facts include identities, event/task/target bindings, ordering, outcome, completion, authorization, and provenance. Abandonment, unethical delegation, responsibility displacement, and regulator conclusions are **REJECTED — SHARED-LAYER SEMANTIC LEAKAGE**.\n\n## Current cases\n\nThe unresolved RAF/TCF cases could only use structural bindings if an authenticated task manager, runtime, environment, or authority adapter supplies fact-specific observations. Prose remains declaration/text-only evidence. `b-036` particularly requires an authoritative stop-event target so `stop(timer)` cannot be confused with `stop(task)`.\n')
